    "result_text": "The full test suite is green: **289 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `locality` field on the owner-creation response. When an owner is created, `locality` is `'local'` if their city is the single most common city among the owners that existed beforehand, otherwise `'remote'`.\n\nChanges made:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added the read-only `locality` string property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added a `@Transient` `locality` field with getter/setter. It's transient (not persisted) because, like `displayName`/`initials`, it only needs to appear in the response; this also avoids schema changes across the four DB dialects. The controller maps the same entity instance that `saveOwner` populates, so the value is present in the creation response.\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../service/ClinicServiceImpl.java`** \u2014 Set `locality` in `saveOwner` (in the `isNew()` block, alongside the other creation-time derived fields) via a new `resolveLocality` method. It groups existing owners by normalized city (case/whitespace-insensitive, consistent with the surrounding code), finds the highest count, and returns `\"local\"` only when there is a *single* city with that highest count and it matches the new owner's city; otherwise `\"remote\"`. No existing owners or a tie for most-common both yield `\"remote\"`.\n\n4. **`src/test/java/.../OwnerRestControllerV1Tests.java`** \u2014 Added two tests: creating an owner in Madison (the single most common seed city, 4 owners) returns `local`; creating one in Sun Prairie (1 owner) returns `remote`.\n\nThe behaviour reflects the state *at creation time* (computed before the new owner is persisted), matching the specification's \"at that time\" wording and the pattern of the sibling fields like `namesakeCount` and `sharesHousehold`.",
